# evidence-cmd: cd plugins/foundry/mcp-server && uv run --quiet --with pytest pytest tests/test_commit_guard.py -v --no-header -p no:cacheprovider
# evidence-for: US-005, AC-015, AC-016, AC-017, FR-012
# evidence-volatile: \d+\.\d+s
# evidence-volatile: Installed \d+ packages? in \d+(\.\d+)?ms
# evidence-timeout: 300
#
# The real-git harness. Every test builds a throwaway repository under
# tmp_path and runs the SHIPPED scripts against it, so each name below is
# an assertion about git as it actually behaves, not about a mock.
#
# AC-015 / OT-005 are the first three: a pathspec commit records only its
# own paths, it leaves a peer's staged file staged, and the bare-commit
# contrast shows why the protocol needs the pathspec at all.
# AC-016 is the guard block. The two tests naming the index as the content
# source pin it from BOTH directions (staged-clean/disk-dirty must pass,
# staged-dirty/disk-clean must block); the path-list tests alone cannot.
# AC-017 / GI-002 are the last four: a static scan of the shipped sources
# proving no executable line stashes or queries the working tree, so a
# future edit reintroducing the anti-pattern fails this suite.
#
# Four tests are new this cycle, locking D-107 — the installer wrote THROUGH
# a symlinked pre-commit hook. `cp` and `chmod` follow a symlink at the
# destination, so against the husky/lefthook layout (`.git/hooks/pre-commit ->
# ../../scripts/my-hook.sh`, tracked) the installer rewrote a TRACKED file in
# the target repo, left the hook unreplaced, and passed its own post-install
# check because `-x` and the marker grep followed the same link.
#
# Three are behavioural and one is structural, and they are chosen so that no
# single lazy fix satisfies them all. `replaces_a_symlinked_hook_without_
# writing_through_it` is the whole defect: the tracked file must be byte-
# identical, `git status` clean, the hook a regular executable file carrying
# the marker, the report must name the link AND resolve it, and the backup
# must still be a link so the printed restore command restores one.
# `no_clobber_refuses_a_symlinked_hook` stops a fix that just unlinks
# everything it finds. `replaces_a_dangling_symlink` covers the case `-e`
# cannot see at all — it follows the link, so a link to a missing path read as
# "nothing here" and took the plain install branch, where `cp` refuses to
# write through a dangling symlink and `set -e` aborted with no named cause.
# The structural one forbids any executable line from copying or redirecting
# onto `"$HOOK_DEST"`, because a bare `cp` reintroduced in the plain-install
# branch would never meet a symlink in the three tests above.
#
# Falsified against the real pre-fix installer read out of git at a5795d2,
# not a hand-reconstruction: exactly these four fail there and all 30
# pre-existing tests pass, so none of them is a tautology.
#
# Five tests were added the previous cycle, locking D-014 — a fail-open the guard had
# at scale. `git cat-file blob :0:$p | grep -q RE` returns a MATCH as a PASS
# on any blob past the pipe buffer: grep -q exits 0 the instant it matches
# and closes the pipe, git takes SIGPIPE and dies 141, `set -o pipefail`
# reports the whole pipeline as 141, and the enclosing `if` reads that
# nonzero status as "no match". Verified against the pre-fix guard: a
# 1.5 MB and a 4.2 MB file, each with a conflict marker on LINE 1, both
# committed cleanly; both are blocked now.
#
# Those four behavioural locks are deliberately paired so neither can be
# satisfied by a lazy fix. The two parametrized sizes stop a fix that only
# enlarges a buffer. `allows_a_large_clean_staged_blob` stops a fix that
# blocks everything big. `exempts_binary_blobs` stops a fix that drops the
# binary probe and starts blocking images. The fifth is structural: no
# executable line may pipe a `cat-file blob` anywhere, so the SHAPE cannot
# return at a size nobody parametrized.

